[ROCm] Keep fp16 tiles held across a loop in registers

uint2 and uint4 are structs in HIP, so a vector copy is a memcpy. SROA leaves a
local array that is only copied to and from memory on the stack, so a tile of
global loads held across a loop in a fragment landed in scratch memory: an fp16
fragment holding 2 rows of K and V per thread took 80 bytes of scratch per lane on
clang 18. Accessed as extended vectors, the same array is promoted to registers:
ScratchSize 0 on clang 22, and on clang 18 a reported 16 bytes with no private
memory instruction in the assembly. The accessors go through
tl::RegVec, which keeps the type's natural alignment, so LDS stores stay
ds_write_b128. Generated C changes for fp16 vector accesses, except on arrays read
as matrix core operands; scalar and fp32 accesses do not change.

Operands stay plain because they are read with GEMM's K loop index. Promoted on
clang 22, such an array leaves the K loop not unrolled and every read becomes a
chain of selects: a paged decode kernel went from 1198 to 4290 instructions and
ran 8.6 to 10.3 times slower with the same output. Clang 18 is not affected.

This makes register staging expressible in TileLang: T.copy from global memory to
a fragment before the V GEMM, T.copy from the fragment to shared memory at the top
of the next K step, with a T.Fragment layout that gives each thread 2 rows.
Measured on MI250X, ROCm 7.2.3, flash attention d256 L=8192, 768 threads, same
process: this form reads 95 TF against 82 for T.copy straight into shared memory.

diff --git a/src/rocm/codegen/codegen_hip.cc b/src/rocm/codegen/codegen_hip.cc
